<h2>Why POCSO settlements do not work like other settlements</h2>
<p>The compounding table in Section 359 BNSS (Section 320 CrPC) is a closed list. Offences under the POCSO Act are not on it, and the Act itself contains no provision for settlement, withdrawal or compromise. That is a deliberate legislative choice: the statute protects children as a class, fixes the age of consent at eighteen, and proceeds on the footing that a child&rsquo;s exploitation cannot be bargained away by adults — including the child&rsquo;s own parents. A prosecution under the Act is conducted by the State, and the complainant family&rsquo;s change of heart does not, by itself, bring it to an end.</p>

<h2>The inherent power — and its discipline</h2>
<p>The High Court&rsquo;s inherent power under Section 528 BNSS (Section 482 CrPC) to quash proceedings survives in every class of case, including POCSO. But the manner of its exercise varies with the nature of the offence. Courts have consistently drawn a line between offences with a predominantly private, civil or commercial flavour — where a genuine settlement often justifies quashing — and offences that are serious, heinous or committed against society at large, where settlement is ordinarily irrelevant. Sexual offences against children sit firmly in the second category.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Private-flavour offences.</strong> Matrimonial cruelty, cheating between business partners, cheque disputes: a real settlement usually ends the matter, because the chance of conviction becomes remote and continuing the trial serves no one.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Offences against society.</strong> Sexual offences, particularly against children, are prosecuted in the public interest. The parties&rsquo; compromise does not diminish the public element, so settlement alone is not a ground to quash.</div>
</div>

<h2>The marriage argument</h2>
<p>A recurring submission is that the accused and the victim have since married, or intend to marry, and that continuing the prosecution would destroy the very household the law should protect. Courts examine such claims with scepticism. The offence is judged as on its date; a later marriage does not convert what the statute defines as an offence into a lawful act, and accepting marriage as a standard exit would create an obvious incentive for pressure on victims and their families. Where courts have shown any flexibility, it has been in a narrow, fact-specific strand of cases involving adolescent relationships close to the age of majority — and even there, intervention turns on the entire record, the victim&rsquo;s clearly expressed position, and the court&rsquo;s own assessment, never on the settlement document alone. The law as enacted remains unchanged: consent below eighteen is no consent.</p>

<h2>What the quashing hearing actually examines</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The record, not the deed.</strong> The court reads the FIR, the statements under Sections 180 and 183 BNSS (161 and 164 CrPC), the medical evidence and the chargesheet. A settlement deed does not answer any of that material; at best it explains the family&rsquo;s present stance.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The victim&rsquo;s position.</strong> The victim — through a guardian where still a minor — is heard. Courts look for signs of pressure, inducement or exhaustion, and may interact with the victim to satisfy themselves that the stand is free and informed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The State&rsquo;s stand.</strong> The prosecution is entitled to oppose, and usually does. The court weighs the realistic prospect of the trial: the age of the victim, the nature of the allegations, and whether the prosecution has independent evidence beyond the complainant family.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The outcome.</strong> If the petition fails, the trial continues with the settlement on record. If it succeeds, the order typically records the exceptional features that justified it — underscoring that the route is not a precedent for routine settlement.</div>
</div>

<h2>Honest advice to the accused&rsquo;s family</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Do not pay for, or rely on, a &ldquo;full and final settlement&rdquo; on the assurance that the case will end. In POCSO matters, no private document can guarantee that.</p>
  <p>Assess the record first: the realistic defence at trial, the quality of the prosecution evidence, and the bail position, before spending the family&rsquo;s resources on a quashing petition with limited prospects.</p>
  <p>Never approach the victim&rsquo;s family directly. Contact can be read as pressure or tampering, can invite cancellation of bail, and can itself become a fresh offence.</p>
  <p>Where the matter involves an adolescent relationship, place the complete, truthful factual matrix before counsel — ages, timeline, the families&rsquo; positions — so that the narrow jurisprudence in that area can be realistically evaluated.</p>
  <p>Treat the trial seriously. In most POCSO cases the real forum is the Special Court, and preparation there matters far more than the hope of a shortcut.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">The POCSO Act reflects a legislative judgment that children&rsquo;s safety is not negotiable, and the courts enforce that judgment. A settlement may bring peace between two families, but it does not, by itself, end a POCSO prosecution. Families on either side of such a case should seek clear, early advice about what the law actually permits — and what it firmly does not.</div>
